1
00:00:07,940 --> 00:00:10,980
And there came a public beggar, who used to beg

2
00:00:10,980 --> 00:00:15,260
through the town of Ithaca,
notorious for his ravening belly,

3
00:00:15,260 --> 00:00:18,740
eating and drinking without end; no strength was in him

4
00:00:18,740 --> 00:00:22,700
and no force, but in bulk he was very big to look at.

5
00:00:22,700 --> 00:00:28,760
Arnaeus was his name — his honored mother gave it
him at birth; but Irus all the young men called him,

6
00:00:28,760 --> 00:00:32,140
because he would run messages whenever anyone bade him.

7
00:00:32,140 --> 00:00:37,660
He came now to drive Odysseus from his own house,
and abusing him he spoke winged words:

8
00:00:37,660 --> 00:00:41,660
"Off the porch, old man —
or be dragged off quick by the foot.

9
00:00:41,660 --> 00:00:47,500
Don't you see how they're all winking at me,
telling me to drag you? I am ashamed to, even so.

10
00:00:47,500 --> 00:00:50,300
Up, then — before our quarrel comes to fists."

11
00:00:50,300 --> 00:00:54,700
Then, glaring from under his brows,
Odysseus of the many designs replied:

12
00:00:54,700 --> 00:01:01,680
"Man possessed — I do you no harm, I say you none;
I grudge no one giving to you, however much he takes up.

13
00:01:01,680 --> 00:01:04,800
This threshold will hold the two of us. You have no need

14
00:01:04,800 --> 00:01:11,360
to grudge what is other men's. You seem to me a wanderer
just as I am — and prosperity is the gods' to give.

15
00:01:11,360 --> 00:01:15,460
Only do not challenge me too far with
your fists, or you will anger me,

16
00:01:15,460 --> 00:01:20,740
and old as I am I will spatter your chest and lips
with blood. And I would have the more peace for it

17
00:01:20,740 --> 00:01:26,460
tomorrow: for I do not think you would come back a
second time to the hall of Laertes' son Odysseus."

18
00:01:26,460 --> 00:01:29,340
Then Irus the vagrant answered him in anger:

19
00:01:29,340 --> 00:01:35,300
"Listen to the glutton — how he rattles on,
like an old woman at her oven! I'll plan him a nasty turn:

20
00:01:35,300 --> 00:01:41,220
hit him with both hands and knock every tooth from
his jaws to the ground, like a crop-raiding sow's.

21
00:01:41,220 --> 00:01:46,220
Belt up now, so all these men may know us as we
fight. But how would you fight a younger man?"

22
00:01:46,220 --> 00:01:48,160
So, before the high doors, on the polished

23
00:01:48,160 --> 00:01:51,660
threshold, the two of them bristled
at each other with whole hearts.

24
00:01:51,660 --> 00:01:54,720
And the sacred strength of Antinous took note of them,

25
00:01:54,720 --> 00:01:58,240
and breaking into easy laughter he called to the suitors:

26
00:01:58,240 --> 00:02:00,600
"Friends — nothing like this has ever happened yet:

27
00:02:00,600 --> 00:02:03,160
what sport the god has brought into this house!

28
00:02:03,160 --> 00:02:09,160
The stranger and Irus are wrangling with each other to
fight it out with fists. Quick — let us match them!"

29
00:02:09,160 --> 00:02:14,280
So he spoke; and they all sprang up laughing
and crowded round the ill-dressed beggars.

30
00:02:14,280 --> 00:02:17,360
And Antinous spoke among them, Eupeithes' son:

31
00:02:17,360 --> 00:02:19,840
"Hear me, lordly suitors, while I say a word.

32
00:02:19,840 --> 00:02:25,740
There are goats' paunches lying at the fire,
which we set by for supper, filled with fat and blood.

33
00:02:25,740 --> 00:02:30,640
Whichever of the two wins and proves the better man,
let him stand up and take his pick of them;

34
00:02:30,640 --> 00:02:36,060
and ever after he shall feast with us,
and no other beggar will we let inside to beg among us."

35
00:02:36,060 --> 00:02:39,460
So spoke Antinous, and his word pleased them well.

36
00:02:39,460 --> 00:02:43,820
Then Odysseus of the many designs,
with craft in mind, spoke among them:

37
00:02:43,820 --> 00:02:50,100
"Friends, there is no way an old man can fight a
younger one — a man worn down by misery. But the belly,

38
00:02:50,100 --> 00:02:54,020
that worker of evil, eggs me on,
to go down under his blows.

39
00:02:54,020 --> 00:02:56,700
Come then, all of you swear me a strong oath:

40
00:02:56,700 --> 00:03:03,940
that no one, doing Irus a favor, will strike me a foul
blow with a heavy hand, and force me under for him."

41
00:03:03,940 --> 00:03:07,360
So he spoke; and they all swore the oath as he demanded.

42
00:03:07,360 --> 00:03:13,200
And when they had sworn and sealed the oath entire,
the sacred strength of Telemachus spoke among them:

43
00:03:13,200 --> 00:03:18,380
"Stranger — if your heart and your proud spirit move
you to beat this man off, fear none of the other

44
00:03:18,380 --> 00:03:21,860
Achaeans: whoever strikes you
will have more than one to fight.

45
00:03:21,860 --> 00:03:28,180
Your host is myself; and the kings second it,
Antinous and Eurymachus, sound-thinking men both."

46
00:03:28,180 --> 00:03:34,980
So he spoke, and they all approved. And Odysseus
girded his rags about his loins, and bared his thighs —

47
00:03:34,980 --> 00:03:40,840
beautiful, massive — and his broad shoulders came
to light, his chest, his heavy arms. And Athena

48
00:03:40,840 --> 00:03:45,360
came close and filled out the limbs
of the shepherd of the people.

49
00:03:45,360 --> 00:03:49,080
And the suitors, every one,
were amazed beyond all measure,

50
00:03:49,080 --> 00:03:51,600
and a man would say, with a glance at his neighbor:

51
00:03:51,600 --> 00:03:55,100
"Irus un-Irused soon — a self-invited beating!

52
00:03:55,100 --> 00:03:58,180
Look what a thigh the old man
shows from under those rags."

53
00:03:58,180 --> 00:04:02,160
So they spoke; and Irus' heart
was stirred — for the worse.

54
00:04:02,160 --> 00:04:08,860
Even so the serving-men girded him and led him out by
force, in terror, the flesh trembling on his limbs.

55
00:04:08,860 --> 00:04:12,460
And Antinous rebuked him, and spoke a word, and named him:

56
00:04:12,460 --> 00:04:15,760
"Better you were not alive,
you ox of a braggart, nor ever born,

57
00:04:15,760 --> 00:04:18,920
if you shiver and fear so terribly before this man —

58
00:04:18,920 --> 00:04:21,960
an old man, worn out with the misery that has found him.

59
00:04:21,960 --> 00:04:24,640
But I will tell you this, and it will be accomplished:

60
00:04:24,640 --> 00:04:27,140
if this man beats you and proves the better man,

61
00:04:27,140 --> 00:04:32,640
I will throw you in a black ship and send you to the
mainland, to King Echetus, the maimer of all mankind,

62
00:04:32,640 --> 00:04:35,760
who will slice away your nose
and ears with the pitiless bronze

63
00:04:35,760 --> 00:04:39,300
and tear off your parts and give
them raw to the dogs to share."

64
00:04:39,300 --> 00:04:43,400
So he spoke; and a still greater
trembling seized Irus' limbs.

65
00:04:43,400 --> 00:04:46,940
But they led him into the middle;
and both men put up their hands.

66
00:04:46,940 --> 00:04:52,360
Then much-enduring noble Odysseus debated whether to
hit him so the life would leave him where he fell,

67
00:04:52,360 --> 00:04:55,700
or to hit him gently and just stretch him on the ground.

68
00:04:55,700 --> 00:04:58,500
And as he weighed it, this seemed to him the better way:

69
00:04:58,500 --> 00:05:02,140
to strike gently, so the Achaeans
would not take note of him.

70
00:05:02,140 --> 00:05:05,880
Then both put up their guard;
Irus drove at the right shoulder,

71
00:05:05,880 --> 00:05:08,740
but he struck the neck under
the ear, and crushed the bones

72
00:05:08,740 --> 00:05:11,800
inward: at once the red blood came up in his mouth,

73
00:05:11,800 --> 00:05:15,660
and he went down bleating in the dust,
teeth clenched together,

74
00:05:15,660 --> 00:05:21,380
heels drumming the ground. And the lordly suitors
threw up their hands and died laughing. Then Odysseus

75
00:05:21,380 --> 00:05:24,380
took him by the foot and dragged him out through the porch

76
00:05:24,380 --> 00:05:27,520
to the courtyard and the colonnade
gates; and there he propped him

77
00:05:27,520 --> 00:05:31,900
against the courtyard fence, sitting up,
and pushed his stick into his hand,

78
00:05:31,900 --> 00:05:34,680
and lifting his voice he spoke winged words to him:

79
00:05:34,680 --> 00:05:40,240
"Sit there now, and keep off the pigs and dogs,
and stop lording it over strangers and beggars,

80
00:05:40,240 --> 00:05:43,220
wretch that you are, or a worse evil yet may find you."

81
00:05:43,220 --> 00:05:49,300
He spoke, and slung about his shoulders his beggar's
pouch, full of holes, hanging by a twisted cord,

82
00:05:49,300 --> 00:05:52,800
and went back to the threshold
and sat down. And they went inside

83
00:05:52,800 --> 00:05:55,800
laughing sweetly, and saluted him with words:

84
00:05:55,800 --> 00:05:59,360
"May Zeus give you, stranger,
and the other deathless gods,

85
00:05:59,360 --> 00:06:04,600
whatever you most desire, whatever is dear to your heart —
you who stopped that insatiable one from begging

86
00:06:04,600 --> 00:06:10,820
through the land! For soon we will haul him to the mainland,
to King Echetus, the maimer of all mankind."

87
00:06:10,820 --> 00:06:14,900
So they spoke; and noble Odysseus
rejoiced at the omen of the words.

88
00:06:14,900 --> 00:06:19,760
And Antinous set beside him the great paunch,
filled with fat and blood; and Amphinomus

89
00:06:19,760 --> 00:06:25,520
took up two loaves from the basket and set them by him,
and pledged him with a golden cup, and said:

90
00:06:25,520 --> 00:06:28,380
"Your health, father stranger! May happiness be yours

91
00:06:28,380 --> 00:06:32,460
in time to come — though now you
are held fast in many evils."

92
00:06:32,460 --> 00:06:35,500
Then Odysseus of the many designs answered him:

93
00:06:35,500 --> 00:06:38,240
"Amphinomus — you seem to me a man of good sense,

94
00:06:38,240 --> 00:06:42,140
and such was the father you come from:
I have heard his good name —

95
00:06:42,140 --> 00:06:45,420
Nisus of Dulichium, a brave man and a rich one.

96
00:06:45,420 --> 00:06:48,520
They say you are his son; and you seem a civil man.

97
00:06:48,520 --> 00:06:51,880
So I will tell you something. Mark it; hear me out.

98
00:06:51,880 --> 00:06:57,080
The earth breeds nothing feebler than a man —
of all things that breathe and move on the face of it.

99
00:06:57,080 --> 00:07:00,260
He tells himself he will never
suffer evil in time to come,

100
00:07:00,260 --> 00:07:03,680
as long as the gods lend him
prowess and his knees are quick;

101
00:07:03,680 --> 00:07:10,300
but when the blessed gods bring hard days too to pass,
these too he bears — unwilling, on an enduring heart.

102
00:07:10,300 --> 00:07:12,740
For such is the mind of men who live on earth:

103
00:07:12,740 --> 00:07:16,600
it is weather — whatever day the
father of gods and men brings on.

104
00:07:16,600 --> 00:07:20,300
I too was once set fair to be fortunate among men,

105
00:07:20,300 --> 00:07:27,700
and I did many reckless things, giving way to my force
and power, trusting in my father and my brothers.

106
00:07:27,700 --> 00:07:34,500
So let no man ever be lawless in anything,
but keep in silence whatever gifts the gods may give.

107
00:07:34,500 --> 00:07:40,600
Such recklessness I see the suitors devising here —
shearing away the goods, dishonoring the wife

108
00:07:40,600 --> 00:07:43,720
of a man who, I tell you, will not long be far

109
00:07:43,720 --> 00:07:48,800
from his friends and his fathers' land.
He is very close. But you —

110
00:07:48,800 --> 00:07:52,640
may your own fate steal you home
instead; may you not meet him

111
00:07:52,640 --> 00:07:55,760
the day he comes back to his own dear country.

112
00:07:55,760 --> 00:07:58,340
For not without blood, I think, will they be parted,

113
00:07:58,340 --> 00:08:01,970
the suitors and that man,
once he comes in under his roof."

114
00:08:01,970 --> 00:08:05,580
So he spoke, and poured, and drank the honey-sweet wine,

115
00:08:05,580 --> 00:08:09,020
then gave the cup back into the
hands of the marshal of the people.

116
00:08:09,020 --> 00:08:11,920
And that man went off down the hall with an aching heart,

117
00:08:11,920 --> 00:08:15,640
head sinking down: for his spirit foresaw the evil.

118
00:08:15,640 --> 00:08:19,960
But even so he did not escape
his fate: Athena bound him too,

119
00:08:19,960 --> 00:08:23,240
to be broken under the hands and spear of Telemachus.

120
00:08:23,240 --> 00:08:26,780
Back he went, and sat down on the
chair from which he had risen.

121
00:08:26,780 --> 00:08:34,100
And now the goddess, gray-eyed Athena, put it in the
mind of Icarius' daughter, watchful-minded Penelope,

122
00:08:34,100 --> 00:08:36,880
to show herself to the suitors: so she might open

123
00:08:36,880 --> 00:08:40,120
their hearts wide as sails,
and come to stand more precious

124
00:08:40,120 --> 00:08:44,560
in her husband's eyes, and her son's,
than she had been before.

125
00:08:44,560 --> 00:08:49,720
She laughed — an empty laugh —
and spoke a word, and named her:

126
00:08:49,720 --> 00:08:55,600
"Eurynome, my heart longs — as it never has before —
to show myself to the suitors, hateful as they are.

127
00:08:55,600 --> 00:08:59,240
And I would say a word to my son — the better for him:

128
00:08:59,240 --> 00:09:05,500
not to keep such company with the overweening suitors,
who speak him fair and mean him evil behind."

129
00:09:05,500 --> 00:09:07,980
And Eurynome the housekeeper answered her:

130
00:09:07,980 --> 00:09:11,260
"Yes, child — all this you have said as it should be.

131
00:09:11,260 --> 00:09:14,760
Go then, and speak your word to your son; hide nothing.

132
00:09:14,760 --> 00:09:17,560
But first wash your skin, and put a shine on your cheeks:

133
00:09:17,560 --> 00:09:23,520
do not go with your face all stained like this with tears.
Grief without respite makes everything worse.

134
00:09:23,520 --> 00:09:29,080
And your son is now the age you prayed the deathless
ones so long to let you see: a bearded man."

135
00:09:29,080 --> 00:09:31,760
Then watchful-minded Penelope answered her:

136
00:09:31,760 --> 00:09:37,680
"Eurynome, do not coax me so — out of love,
I know — to wash my skin and put a gloss upon it.

137
00:09:37,680 --> 00:09:43,980
Whatever bloom I had, the gods who hold Olympus destroyed
it the day that man went off in the hollow ships.

138
00:09:43,980 --> 00:09:48,320
But tell Autonoe and Hippodamia to
come and stand beside me in the hall:

139
00:09:48,320 --> 00:09:51,000
alone I will not go among men. I would be ashamed."

140
00:09:51,000 --> 00:09:57,380
So she spoke; and the old woman went off through the
hall to carry word to the women and hurry them along.

141
00:09:57,380 --> 00:10:00,680
Then the goddess, gray-eyed Athena,
thought of one thing more:

142
00:10:00,680 --> 00:10:07,020
over Icarius' daughter she poured sweet sleep,
and she slept, leaning back, and all her joints went loose

143
00:10:07,020 --> 00:10:10,300
right there on the couch.
And meanwhile the shining goddess

144
00:10:10,300 --> 00:10:13,740
was giving her immortal gifts, to make the Achaeans stare.

145
00:10:13,740 --> 00:10:18,260
she cleansed her lovely face with the
ambrosial balm that crowned Cytherea

146
00:10:18,260 --> 00:10:21,880
anoints herself with, when she
goes to the Graces' lovely dance;

147
00:10:21,880 --> 00:10:27,220
and she made her taller and fuller to the eye,
and made her whiter than sawn ivory.

148
00:10:27,220 --> 00:10:32,880
That done, the shining goddess went away,
and the white-armed handmaids came in from the hall,

149
00:10:32,880 --> 00:10:36,280
talking as they came; and the sweet sleep let her go.

150
00:10:36,280 --> 00:10:38,840
She rubbed her cheeks with her hands and said:

151
00:10:38,840 --> 00:10:43,120
"Soft — the softest of trances
wrapped me round, for all my anguish.

152
00:10:43,120 --> 00:10:46,040
If only pure Artemis would give me a death as soft,

153
00:10:46,040 --> 00:10:50,180
this very instant — so that I
need no longer mourn at heart,

154
00:10:50,180 --> 00:10:52,420
wasting my life away, longing for my dear husband

155
00:10:52,420 --> 00:10:56,860
and his every excellence:
for he stood out among the Achaeans."

156
00:10:56,860 --> 00:11:02,640
So speaking she came down from her shining upper room —
not alone: two handmaids followed with her.

157
00:11:02,640 --> 00:11:08,900
And when she reached the suitors, shining among women,
she stood beside the pillar of the close-built roof,

158
00:11:08,900 --> 00:11:14,480
holding her glistening veil before her cheeks,
a trusted handmaid standing on either side.

159
00:11:14,480 --> 00:11:19,120
And their knees went slack on the spot,
their hearts spellbound with desire,

160
00:11:19,120 --> 00:11:22,160
and every man prayed to lie in bed beside her.

161
00:11:22,160 --> 00:11:25,700
But she spoke to Telemachus, her dear son:

162
00:11:25,700 --> 00:11:29,900
"Telemachus, your mind is no
longer steady, nor your thought.

163
00:11:29,900 --> 00:11:34,100
While you were still a boy,
you turned shrewder plans than this;

164
00:11:34,100 --> 00:11:37,760
but now that you are grown,
come to the measure of manhood,

165
00:11:37,760 --> 00:11:43,060
so that a stranger, looking at your height and beauty,
would say you must be some rich man's son —

166
00:11:43,060 --> 00:11:46,440
your mind and thought are no longer as they should be.

167
00:11:46,440 --> 00:11:48,860
What a thing has been done here in the halls:

168
00:11:48,860 --> 00:11:51,540
you let the stranger be manhandled so!

169
00:11:51,540 --> 00:11:56,460
What now — if a stranger, sitting in our house,
should come to harm from such ugly hauling-about?

170
00:11:56,460 --> 00:12:00,300
On you the shame and the disgrace
would fall, among all men."

171
00:12:00,300 --> 00:12:03,160
Then clear-headed Telemachus answered her:

172
00:12:03,160 --> 00:12:06,660
"Mother mine, I cannot resent your anger at this.

173
00:12:06,660 --> 00:12:09,780
But I do take note in my heart, I do know each thing,

174
00:12:09,780 --> 00:12:13,860
the good and the worse alike. Before, I was still a child.

175
00:12:13,860 --> 00:12:16,980
Yet I cannot manage to think out everything wisely:

176
00:12:16,980 --> 00:12:23,040
these men unhinge me — crowding me this side and that,
plotting their mischief; and I have no helpers.

177
00:12:23,040 --> 00:12:28,860
Still, the bout of the stranger and Irus did not go as
the suitors intended: the stranger was the stronger.

178
00:12:28,860 --> 00:12:33,420
If only — father Zeus, Athena, Apollo! —
the suitors in our house would nod their heads

179
00:12:33,420 --> 00:12:37,180
like that, beaten and broken, some in the courtyard,

180
00:12:37,180 --> 00:12:42,800
some inside the house, and the limbs of each gone slack,
the way that Irus over there at the courtyard gates

181
00:12:42,800 --> 00:12:47,940
sits lolling his head like a man dead drunk,
and cannot stand upright on his feet, or go

182
00:12:47,940 --> 00:12:51,940
home, wherever home is — since his own limbs are slack."

183
00:12:51,940 --> 00:12:54,760
So they spoke to one another of such things.

184
00:12:54,760 --> 00:12:57,520
And Eurymachus addressed Penelope with words:

185
00:12:57,520 --> 00:13:03,720
"Daughter of Icarius, watchful-minded Penelope,
if all the Achaeans through Iasian Argos could see you,

186
00:13:03,720 --> 00:13:08,680
more suitors yet would be feasting in your house
from tomorrow morning: for you surpass all women

187
00:13:08,680 --> 00:13:12,840
in form and stature, and in the balanced mind within."

188
00:13:12,840 --> 00:13:15,280
Then watchful-minded Penelope answered him:

189
00:13:15,280 --> 00:13:21,160
"Eurymachus, my excellence — my face, my figure —
the deathless ones destroyed the day the Argives

190
00:13:21,160 --> 00:13:24,260
boarded ship for Ilium, and my husband Odysseus with them.

191
00:13:24,260 --> 00:13:30,300
If he came back — if he watched over this life of
mine — my fame would be greater so, and lovelier.

192
00:13:30,300 --> 00:13:34,300
Now I only grieve: so many evils the god has driven at me.

193
00:13:34,300 --> 00:13:39,540
Truly, when he went, leaving his fathers' land,
he took me by the right hand at the wrist and said:

194
00:13:39,540 --> 00:13:44,220
'Wife — I do not think the strong-greaved Achaeans
will all come home from Troy unharmed and whole:

195
00:13:44,220 --> 00:13:49,260
the Trojans too, they say, are men of war,
spear-throwers, drawers of the bow,

196
00:13:49,260 --> 00:13:54,740
mounters of swift-footed horses — such as
decide soonest the great strife of leveling war.

197
00:13:54,740 --> 00:13:57,500
So I cannot know whether the god will bring me back,

198
00:13:57,500 --> 00:14:01,500
or I go down there in Troy.
Let everything here be your care.

199
00:14:01,500 --> 00:14:07,080
Remember my father and my mother in these halls
as now, or even more, while I am far away.

200
00:14:07,080 --> 00:14:12,500
But when you see our son a bearded man,
marry whom you will, and leave your house behind.'

201
00:14:12,500 --> 00:14:15,820
So he spoke it, that man.
And now it is all being accomplished:

202
00:14:15,820 --> 00:14:18,460
the night will come when the
marriage I loathe will meet me —

203
00:14:18,460 --> 00:14:23,060
my cursed self, whose happiness Zeus has torn away.

204
00:14:23,060 --> 00:14:26,100
But this bitter shame comes over my heart and spirit:

205
00:14:26,100 --> 00:14:28,880
this is not what suitors' custom used to be.

206
00:14:28,880 --> 00:14:34,260
Men who would woo a good woman, a rich man's daughter,
and contend with one another for her —

207
00:14:34,260 --> 00:14:36,900
they drive up cattle of their own, and fat sheep,

208
00:14:36,900 --> 00:14:41,020
a feast for the bride's people,
and they give her shining gifts.

209
00:14:41,020 --> 00:14:44,480
They do not devour another man's living, scot-free."

210
00:14:44,480 --> 00:14:48,020
So she spoke; and much-enduring noble Odysseus was glad

211
00:14:48,020 --> 00:14:50,680
that she drew gifts out of them, charming their hearts

212
00:14:50,680 --> 00:14:54,860
with honeyed words, while her
mind was bent on other things.

213
00:14:54,860 --> 00:14:58,740
Then Antinous answered her, Eupeithes' son:

214
00:14:58,740 --> 00:15:04,540
"Daughter of Icarius, watchful-minded Penelope,
the gifts — whatever any Achaean wishes to bring here,

215
00:15:04,540 --> 00:15:07,700
accept them: it is not good manners to refuse a gift.

216
00:15:07,700 --> 00:15:14,740
But we are not going to our lands, nor anywhere else,
until you marry whichever of the Achaeans is best."

217
00:15:14,740 --> 00:15:21,460
So spoke Antinous, and his word pleased them well,
and each man sent a herald off to bring his gifts.

218
00:15:21,460 --> 00:15:26,900
For Antinous he brought a great embroidered robe,
beautiful, and in it were brooches, twelve in all,

219
00:15:26,900 --> 00:15:30,460
of gold, fitted with well-bent clasps.

220
00:15:30,460 --> 00:15:33,080
For Eurymachus one brought a necklace, richly worked

221
00:15:33,080 --> 00:15:37,620
in gold, strung with pieces of amber, bright as the sun.

222
00:15:37,620 --> 00:15:40,660
For Eurydamas two henchmen brought a pair of earrings,

223
00:15:40,660 --> 00:15:44,060
triple-dropped like mulberries;
and much grace glinted from them.

224
00:15:44,060 --> 00:15:48,120
And out of the house of lord Peisander, Polyctor's son,

225
00:15:48,120 --> 00:15:51,420
a henchman brought a neck-band, a jewel of great beauty.

226
00:15:51,420 --> 00:15:54,860
And so one Achaean after another brought his lovely gift.

227
00:15:54,860 --> 00:15:58,160
Then she went up to her upper room, shining among women,

228
00:15:58,160 --> 00:16:01,200
and her handmaids carried the beautiful gifts beside her.

229
00:16:01,200 --> 00:16:04,180
But the men turned to the dance and to lovely song

230
00:16:04,180 --> 00:16:07,660
and took their pleasure there,
and waited for evening to come.

231
00:16:07,660 --> 00:16:11,220
And as they took their pleasure,
the black evening came upon them.

232
00:16:11,220 --> 00:16:17,340
At once they set up three braziers in the halls to
give them light, and heaped dry kindling round —

233
00:16:17,340 --> 00:16:20,800
long-seasoned, bone-dry, new-split with the bronze —

234
00:16:20,800 --> 00:16:24,480
and mixed in pine-brands. And turn by turn the slave-women

235
00:16:24,480 --> 00:16:28,320
of enduring-hearted Odysseus
kept the flame alive. And to them

236
00:16:28,320 --> 00:16:32,400
he himself spoke, Zeus-born Odysseus of the many designs:

237
00:16:32,400 --> 00:16:36,020
"Slave-women of Odysseus, of your master so long gone:

238
00:16:36,020 --> 00:16:41,220
go through to the rooms where the honored queen is;
turn the spindle at her side, and cheer her heart,

239
00:16:41,220 --> 00:16:44,600
sitting there in her chamber,
or card the wool with your hands.

240
00:16:44,600 --> 00:16:47,500
I will keep up the light for all these men myself.

241
00:16:47,500 --> 00:16:53,940
Even if they choose to wait for fair-throned Dawn,
they will not outlast me. I am a very patient man."

242
00:16:53,940 --> 00:16:57,760
So he spoke; and they burst out
laughing, glancing at one another.

243
00:16:57,760 --> 00:17:03,400
And fair-cheeked Melantho jeered at him shamefully —
Dolius fathered her, but Penelope brought her up

244
00:17:03,400 --> 00:17:07,840
and cherished her like her own child,
and gave her toys to her heart's desire;

245
00:17:07,840 --> 00:17:13,860
yet even so no grief for Penelope touched her mind,
but she slept with Eurymachus, and loved him.

246
00:17:13,860 --> 00:17:16,720
She now jeered at Odysseus with words of abuse:

247
00:17:16,720 --> 00:17:19,820
"You sorry stranger — you must be some kind of half-wit:

248
00:17:19,820 --> 00:17:22,100
you will not go and sleep at the smithy's house,

249
00:17:22,100 --> 00:17:25,760
or some public shed — no,
you stand here running your mouth,

250
00:17:25,760 --> 00:17:28,600
bold as brass among all these men, and your heart feels

251
00:17:28,600 --> 00:17:32,300
no fear. Either the wine has
hold of your wits, or your mind

252
00:17:32,300 --> 00:17:35,820
is always of this sort — that is why you babble so idly.

253
00:17:35,820 --> 00:17:38,780
Or are you giddy because you beat that vagrant Irus?

254
00:17:38,780 --> 00:17:41,820
Watch out that no better man than Irus stands up soon,

255
00:17:41,820 --> 00:17:44,720
who will box both sides of your skull with his heavy hands

256
00:17:44,720 --> 00:17:47,380
and send you out of the house all fouled with blood."

257
00:17:47,380 --> 00:17:51,960
Then, glaring from under his brows,
Odysseus of the many designs replied:

258
00:17:51,960 --> 00:17:55,440
"One word to Telemachus, you bitch,
and I go straight to him now,

259
00:17:55,440 --> 00:18:00,980
for what you are saying — and he will
cut you limb from limb on the spot."

260
00:18:00,980 --> 00:18:04,360
So speaking, he set the women scattering in terror:

261
00:18:04,360 --> 00:18:06,860
off they went through the house,
and the knees of every one

262
00:18:06,860 --> 00:18:10,020
went slack with fear; they thought he spoke the truth.

263
00:18:10,020 --> 00:18:12,720
But he took his stand by the burning braziers, tending

264
00:18:12,720 --> 00:18:15,760
the light, and watched them all;
and other things his heart

265
00:18:15,760 --> 00:18:19,740
was pondering deep within —
things not destined to fall short.

266
00:18:19,740 --> 00:18:25,000
And Athena would not let the lordly suitors hold back
from heart-stinging outrage — so that pain might sink

267
00:18:25,000 --> 00:18:28,020
still deeper into Laertes' son Odysseus.

268
00:18:28,020 --> 00:18:31,560
So Eurymachus, Polybus' son, began to speak among them,

269
00:18:31,560 --> 00:18:35,320
baiting Odysseus; and he raised
a laugh among his companions:

270
00:18:35,320 --> 00:18:40,900
"Hear me, you suitors of the far-famed queen,
while I say what the heart in my breast commands.

271
00:18:40,900 --> 00:18:45,440
Not without god's guidance has
this man come to Odysseus' house —

272
00:18:45,440 --> 00:18:48,200
at any rate the blaze of the torches seems to me to come

273
00:18:48,200 --> 00:18:52,680
straight off his head: for there
is not one hair on it, not a wisp."

274
00:18:52,680 --> 00:18:55,960
He spoke, and turned to Odysseus, sacker of cities:

275
00:18:55,960 --> 00:18:58,700
"Stranger — would you take a hire, if I took you on,

276
00:18:58,700 --> 00:19:04,560
out at the end of my lands — your pay will not fail you —
picking stones for walls and planting tall trees?

277
00:19:04,560 --> 00:19:06,760
There I would furnish you bread the year round,

278
00:19:06,760 --> 00:19:10,240
and put clothes on your body,
and give you sandals for your feet.

279
00:19:10,240 --> 00:19:13,160
But no: you have learned bad ways, and you will not

280
00:19:13,160 --> 00:19:16,060
set yourself to work; you would
rather cringe through the town

281
00:19:16,060 --> 00:19:19,380
begging, to feed that bottomless belly of yours."

282
00:19:19,380 --> 00:19:22,260
Then Odysseus of the many designs answered him:

283
00:19:22,260 --> 00:19:25,420
"Eurymachus — if only the two of
us could have a match at work,

284
00:19:25,420 --> 00:19:28,060
in the spring season, when the days grow long:

285
00:19:28,060 --> 00:19:32,900
at mowing — I with a well-curved scythe in hand,
and you with one like it — a trial of work,

286
00:19:32,900 --> 00:19:36,240
fasting right up to dark,
and the grass still standing thick.

287
00:19:36,240 --> 00:19:42,220
Or oxen to drive — the very best oxen,
tawny and big, both of them fat with grass,

288
00:19:42,220 --> 00:19:48,280
matched in age and pull, their strength no trifle,
and a four-acre field, the soil giving under the plow:

289
00:19:48,280 --> 00:19:51,560
then you would see whether I could
cut a furrow straight and long.

290
00:19:51,560 --> 00:19:57,960
Or if the son of Cronos stirred up war — from anywhere —
this very day, and I had a shield and two spears

291
00:19:57,960 --> 00:20:01,080
and a helmet all of bronze, fitted to my temples:

292
00:20:01,080 --> 00:20:03,500
then you would see me mixed into the front fighters,

293
00:20:03,500 --> 00:20:06,920
and you would not talk and talk,
jeering at this belly of mine.

294
00:20:06,920 --> 00:20:10,540
But no — you are full of insolence, and your mind is hard,

295
00:20:10,540 --> 00:20:13,900
and no doubt you fancy yourself a big man and a mighty,

296
00:20:13,900 --> 00:20:16,820
because you keep company with few — and none of them good.

297
00:20:16,820 --> 00:20:22,180
But if Odysseus came — if he reached his own country —
those doors, wide as they are, would all at once

298
00:20:22,180 --> 00:20:25,760
be too narrow for you,
flying through the porch for the street."

299
00:20:25,760 --> 00:20:29,240
So he spoke; and Eurymachus grew the angrier at heart,

300
00:20:29,240 --> 00:20:32,340
and glaring from under his brows he spoke winged words:

301
00:20:32,340 --> 00:20:35,880
"Wretch — I will finish you off
for good, for the way you talk,

302
00:20:35,880 --> 00:20:38,800
bold as brass among all these men, and your heart feels

303
00:20:38,800 --> 00:20:42,300
no fear. Either the wine has
hold of your wits, or your mind

304
00:20:42,300 --> 00:20:45,820
is always of this sort — that is why you babble so idly.

305
00:20:45,820 --> 00:20:48,540
Or are you giddy because you beat that vagrant Irus?"

306
00:20:48,540 --> 00:20:54,100
With that he seized a footstool. But Odysseus
sank down at the knees of Amphinomus of Dulichium,

307
00:20:54,100 --> 00:20:57,060
in fear of Eurymachus; and the stool struck the cupbearer

308
00:20:57,060 --> 00:21:00,580
on the right hand: his wine-jug
rang on the floor where it fell,

309
00:21:00,580 --> 00:21:03,280
and the man groaned and went over backward in the dust.

310
00:21:03,280 --> 00:21:09,160
And the suitors raised a din through the shadowy halls,
and a man would say, with a glance at his neighbor:

311
00:21:09,160 --> 00:21:12,000
"If only the stranger had died
on his wanderings somewhere else

312
00:21:12,000 --> 00:21:15,420
before he ever came! He would
not have brought all this uproar.

313
00:21:15,420 --> 00:21:18,740
Now here we are brawling over
beggars; there will be no joy

314
00:21:18,740 --> 00:21:22,480
even in the good feast, now the worse is winning out."

315
00:21:22,480 --> 00:21:25,920
And the sacred strength of Telemachus spoke among them:

316
00:21:25,920 --> 00:21:29,460
"You are possessed — you have
gone mad. You can no longer hide

317
00:21:29,460 --> 00:21:33,200
what you have eaten and drunk. Some god is goading you on.

318
00:21:33,200 --> 00:21:36,640
No — you have feasted well: now go home and lie down,

319
00:21:36,640 --> 00:21:40,360
whenever the spirit moves you.
Not that I am driving anyone out."

320
00:21:40,360 --> 00:21:46,220
So he spoke; and they all bit their lips and stared
in wonder at Telemachus, that he spoke so boldly.

321
00:21:46,220 --> 00:21:52,560
Then Amphinomus rose and spoke among them —
the shining son of Nisus, of the lord Aretias' line:

322
00:21:52,560 --> 00:21:57,740
"Friends — no man should take offense at a just word
spoken, and answer it with wrangling and hard speech.

323
00:21:57,740 --> 00:22:02,940
Do not knock the stranger about, nor any other
of the slaves in the house of godlike Odysseus.

324
00:22:02,940 --> 00:22:08,320
Come — let the cupbearer pour first-drops in the cups,
so we can make libation and go home to bed;

325
00:22:08,320 --> 00:22:12,420
and the stranger — let us leave
him in the halls of Odysseus

326
00:22:12,420 --> 00:22:17,260
for Telemachus to see to:
it is his dear house the man has come to."

327
00:22:17,260 --> 00:22:20,640
So he spoke, and the word he spoke pleased all of them.

328
00:22:20,640 --> 00:22:27,080
And the hero Moulius mixed the bowl for them,
the herald from Dulichium, Amphinomus' henchman;

329
00:22:27,080 --> 00:22:33,200
he served it round to all in turn; and they poured
to the blessed gods, and drank the honey-sweet wine.

330
00:22:33,200 --> 00:22:36,800
And when they had poured, and drunk
as much as their hearts would have,

331
00:22:36,800 --> 00:22:40,608
they went their ways to bed, each man to his own house.
